<!--
    The Swift concurrency runtime's prototypes and the async context of a
    function that calls none of it (`option swiftcall on|off`).

    Issue: `swift_task_switch` and the other suspension entry points are
    `swiftasync` functions that take the caller's context in the swiftasync
    register (r14 on x86-64, x22 on arm64); with no prototype the call showed
    the resume function and the executor but not the context it resumes
    with. A resume partial that only loads its frame and returns through the
    context calls no task runtime, so nothing said it is `async`: it read r14
    as an unnamed register, and its return, a jump to the context's resume
    function, did not pass the context.

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 `swiftruntime` (the generated concurrency table,
    `kuna_swiftconcurrency.rs`) and P4 prototype models
    (`kuna_swiftinfer.rs`: `seed` on the swiftasync register,
    `async_callee_model`, `async_tail_call`, `passes_context`).

    P0 assertion: `option swiftcall on|off` (default on).

    Both passes load the stripped `swift_async_elf` with `swiftmetadata on`.
    Pass 1 (`option swiftcall off`): `resumeLater(_:)` (0x5200) hands
    `swift_task_switch` no context, and the partial of `hop(_:_:)` (0x4110)
    reads r14 unnamed. Pass 2 (the default): the entry and the partial take
    `async_context`, `swift_task_switch` and the return through
    `resume_parent` pass it, and the continuation in 0x3700 is typed.
    The last two passes load the stripped `swift_sigs_elf`, whose `Box.slow`
    (0x3ea0) awaits the imported `Task.yield()`: only the import's mangled
    name says it is `async`, so only with `swiftcall` on is the call handed
    the context allocated for it.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
